WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:04.880
SARA: A child in any given moment just needs to be loved

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:09.360
and when nobody else is there&nbsp;
to provide it, someone has to.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:15.920
And it doesn't matter who it is or how it is, they&nbsp;
should be able to have the love that they deserve.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:33.280
I have always wanted to be a foster parent as&nbsp;
long as I can remember, from probably about 12

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:38.201
years old on, and my joy and my passion&nbsp;
is children.

00:00:38.201 --> 00:00:40.000
Ah you do match with me!

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:47.031
So for me, it just made sense to take&nbsp;
care of children in the community in that way.

00:00:47.031 --> 00:00:52.560
NICK: You know, initially, she&nbsp;wanted
lots of kids and I wanted one. [laughs]

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:58.240
Little did I know then, like, I couldn't&nbsp;
fathom that it would grow to what it is now.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:11.360
I don't think that I ever went out&nbsp;
looking to have a multi-racial family,

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:15.840
but I don't think that kids growing&nbsp;
up in a multi-racial household look

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:19.584
at their brothers or sisters as anything&nbsp;
different than their brothers or sisters.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:34.800
NAOMI: The Multiethnic Placement Act bars child welfare&nbsp;
agencies, adoption agencies from discriminating

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:42.960
on the basis of race or ethnicity in placing&nbsp;
kids for foster care or adoption.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:51.040
The idea was: Kids should not languish in foster care
when there are loving, safe homes out there for them.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:57.120
And what you had was a situation where there&nbsp;
were a lot of black children in the foster care

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:05.306
system and caseworkers, lawyers, judges were not&nbsp;
placing those black kids in  those&nbsp;loving, safe homes

00:02:05.306 --> 00:02:09.833
because their skin color&nbsp;did not
match the skin color of the parents

00:02:09.833 --> 00:02:16.340
willing to take them in. And so a group of&nbsp;
bipartisan people in congress got together

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:22.713
and said "This is crazy. What these children need&nbsp;
are families, and it doesn't matter whether they're

00:02:22.713 --> 00:02:28.240
white families or black families or hispanic&nbsp;
families. What they need is a loving, safe home."

00:02:28.935 --> 00:02:33.815
Unfortunately, the Multiethnic Placement&nbsp;
Act is becoming increasingly unpopular,

00:02:33.815 --> 00:02:40.855
and I am just amazed at the way these activists&nbsp;
from outside the system are using the foster care

00:02:40.855 --> 00:02:45.161
system to advance an agenda of
social justice that&nbsp;has absolutely

00:02:45.161 --> 00:02:48.039
nothing to do with the needs of these children.

00:02:49.668 --> 00:02:56.318
NICK: Astrid, go eat your breakfast.
LILA ASTRID: Okay.

00:02:56.626 --> 00:03:02.709
NAOMI: So, Sarah and Nick Pendleton have adopted four children. Two of the children that they adopted

00:03:02.709 --> 00:03:13.047
were African-American, and I cannot figure out why
we would care what Nick and Sarah's skin&nbsp;color is

00:03:13.047 --> 00:03:22.003
when it comes to their ability to care for these children.
SARA: I had just had this dream, just about a little girl with

00:03:22.003 --> 00:03:28.618
beautiful brown skin and heart problems, and when the worker called, she said "I have a girl."

00:03:28.618 --> 00:03:32.646
It was like, "boom." I knew she was my girl,

00:03:32.668 --> 00:03:38.215
and she was like, "Well, i need to tell you&nbsp;about her."
And I was like, "Okay." And she said "She's&nbsp;really sick

00:03:38.215 --> 00:03:43.456
and she's got some heart defects." And&nbsp;
the nurse came in and laid a long list of all the&nbsp;things

00:03:43.456 --> 00:03:49.437
wrong with her on the table, and they were&nbsp;all
looking at me like, "Okay you're gonna walk out&nbsp;too,"

00:03:49.437 --> 00:03:55.885
because so many people had turned down this child.
NICK:  Even with a medical background, I wasn't

00:03:55.885 --> 00:04:02.365
anticipating the level of care that she would&nbsp;
need, but I think over time, our family just kind of

00:04:02.365 --> 00:04:07.501
evolved into a family that can handle those&nbsp;
different things when they come up.

00:04:07.501 --> 00:04:14.405
NAOMI: She was such a part of their family. Even though
they knew that her situation was terminal,

00:04:14.405 --> 00:04:23.162
she would go in a wagon places with them; the other kids&nbsp;
would get into bed with her and, like, talk to her.

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:31.830
So, Ainsley was seven when she finally died.
She had been to Disney World and Lincoln Center;

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:40.717
she had been caving... All of these things that&nbsp; they did as
a family together to cement&nbsp;their relationship with her,

00:04:40.717 --> 00:04:45.510
and it is the kind&nbsp;of life that
any child deserves, and especially

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:48.901
a child who has been through this kind of trauma.

00:04:49.974 --> 00:04:55.076
SARA: As the mom to two black children, I see their race,

00:04:55.076 --> 00:05:04.571
but I see them more than any skin&nbsp;
color, hair color, hair type... I see them.

00:05:04.571 --> 00:05:11.761
It's heartbreaking to imagine that either of&nbsp;
those girls would still be without a family.

00:05:25.021 --> 00:05:29.661
NICK: Astrid does not have any thought about the fact&nbsp;
that she's living with white parents, but she does

00:05:29.661 --> 00:05:35.396
know that she's loved, and she has permanency here,&nbsp;
and she knows nobody's taking her away from here.

00:05:35.396 --> 00:05:42.126
One day, will she ask me about race? "Why am I...
do I have dark skin and you have white skin?"

00:05:42.126 --> 00:05:47.501
Sure, she's gonna ask me about that, but the&nbsp;
one thing she will not ever have to wonder is,

00:05:47.501 --> 00:05:51.617
"Is somebody gonna come take me tonight and&nbsp;
move me somewhere else?" She'll never have that

00:05:51.617 --> 00:05:57.231
question, because what it comes down to at the end&nbsp;
of the day is that she's loved and she has a place.

00:05:58.078 --> 00:06:02.347
NAOMI: I think what the Pendletons&nbsp;
have provided to their children

00:06:02.347 --> 00:06:10.398
is so vital, and I think all the time about&nbsp;
how we can find more families like them.

00:06:13.316 --> 00:06:17.099
And what I see is a system that&nbsp;
is working to push that away;

00:06:17.588 --> 00:06:25.976
a system that is working to say to Nick, "You're a
racist because you think skin color doesn't matter."

00:06:25.976 --> 00:06:31.846
I think that's the opposite of where we need to&nbsp;
be as a country, and I look at the Pendletons, and

00:06:31.846 --> 00:06:38.406
I think they should be leading us in terms of what&nbsp;
we're looking for in foster and adoptive families.

00:06:38.406 --> 00:06:49.526
And whatever Nick Pendleton has to say is so much&nbsp;
more important and so much more true than the

00:06:49.526 --> 00:06:55.920
nonsense that is being spouted by these activists&nbsp;
who don't even understand what these children need.

00:06:57.222 --> 00:07:04.742
SARA: Every child deserves the same as the&nbsp;next.
Period. End of story. And whatever it&nbsp;takes

00:07:04.742 --> 00:07:10.822
to give every child a family; to give&nbsp;
every child an advocate who thinks they hung

00:07:10.822 --> 00:07:14.982
the moon and the stars... Whether they're going&nbsp;
to live, whether they're going to die, whether

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:19.320
they can read or write, whether they can walk,&nbsp;
whether they're even going to blink their eyes

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:25.442
one time on this entire earth, whether they live&nbsp;
for one week or the rest of their parent's life...

00:07:27.316 --> 00:07:33.747
Every child deserves to have someone who&nbsp;
thinks that they are the greatest on this earth.

